==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[Image:Handcuffs01 2003-06-02.jpg|thumb|right|A pair of handcuffs.]]

===Etymology 1===
1775, from {{compound|en|hand|cuff|t2=end of shirtsleeve}}.<ref name="oetlyd">{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

Possibly influenced by {{inh|en|ang|handcops}}, from {{m|ang|hand}} + {{m|ang|cops||fetter, chains}}, but due to lack of continuity (centuries between Old English and modern term), generally analyzed as a re-invention.<ref name="oetlyd" />

====Noun====
{{en-noun|p}}

# {{senseid|en|Q15808}} A [[fastening]] consisting of two [[metal]] [[ring]]s, designed to go around a person's [[wrist]]s, and connected by a [[chain]] or [[hinge]].
#: {{cot|en|shackles#Noun|straitjacket#Noun}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Great Expectations|I|V|61|It was the sergeant who had spoken to me, and he was now looking round at the company, with his '''handcuffs''' invitingly extended towards them in his right hand, and his left on my shoulder.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ian Black|title=Courts kept busy as Jordan works to crush support for Isis|titleurl=http://www.theguardian.com/world/2014/nov/27/-sp-courts-jordan-crush-support-isis|journal=The Guardian|date=27 November 2014
|passage=Security is tight inside and outside the building, guarded by a bewildering collection of soldiers, policemen and gendarmes. Relatives watch as prisoners in '''handcuffs''' and leg irons shuffle past.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} Any [[abstract#Adjective|abstract]] [[constraint]], such as rules, regulations, or lack of resources.
#: {{cot|en|shackles#Noun|straitjacket#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|They complained that their rules of engagement were '''handcuffs''' that guaranteed counterproductivity.}}

